#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A workflow's door, before and after its harness session (solorepo's DR-264).

Usage:
    .meta/say/on --role reviewer reviewer before <n>
    .meta/say/on --role reviewer reviewer between <n> --attempt N --outcome <outcome> \\
        [--transcript <path>]
    .meta/say/on --role reviewer reviewer after <n>
    .meta/say/on --role reviewer reviewer after <pr> --verdicts N --agents N \\
        --ran claude|gemini|jules|none [--transcript <path>]
    .meta/say/on coder before <n> --pass take|rebase|answer|promote --event <event> \\
        [--harness <harness>]
    .meta/say/on coder between <n> --pass take|rebase|answer|promote --event <event> \\
        [--attempt N --outcome <outcome>] [--execution-file <path>]
    .meta/say/on coder after <n> --pass take|rebase|answer|promote --event <event> \\
        --outcomes <outcome>[,<outcome>] [--count N] [--branch-prefix <harness>] \\
        [--execution-file <path>]

Each loop workflow is a door a Role's run arrives at: `triage.yml` and
`review.yml` are the reviewer's, `coder.yml` the coder's. What the door's shell
steps read and decided around the harness session is typed here as one program
of the channel, so that a probe can hold it and the YAML keeps its triggers,
concurrency, permissions, credentials, and the harness steps. The verb names
the Role whose door it is, and `--role` names the credential that reads
GitHub, which is that Role's: `channel.parser` defaults it to the coder's, so
the reviewer's door is typed with the flag. `before` makes every read and
decision the session needs and writes them as `key=value` lines to
`GITHUB_OUTPUT`, and the harness's name to `GITHUB_ENV` under `ACTOR_AGENT`,
the name the channel reads in a run, beside the `AI_AGENT` the harness
overwrites with its own build string (solorepo's DR-233), and writes the files
under `.review/` the session reads; `after` reads what the session left. The
number says which of the Role's doors this is: a Challenge is the reading
door, `triage.yml`, and a pull request is the review door, `review.yml`. The
coder's door, `coder.yml`, has four passes behind it and the workflow says
which with `--pass`, since the event, the review's state and the dispatch's
inputs are the workflow's to read; its `after` takes how each rung of the
pass ended, in order, refused absent by the same rule as the review door's
numbers below, and hands back a take that did not finish, redelivers the
review pass a rebase cleared, and ends the run red where a rung failed a
pass and none finished it, a cancelled pass not being a failed one. A
take pass cut by its turn cap is one that did not finish and says so nowhere
in its step conclusion, which `claude-code-action` reports as `success` when
the model's turn ends at the cap; `--execution-file` names the transcript the
harness writes the reason in, and the door reads the cap off it
(solorepo's DR-277). Every pass has a third phase, `between`, which runs
between two rungs of the harness ladder and on the next rung's own
condition: told which rung ended and how, it says whether the next runs and
writes that rung's prompt, and on a take it reads the branch again so that
the prompt says what is open on it now rather than what `before` read of it
ahead of every rung (solorepo's DR-281). The reviewer's door has the same
phase, for the review ladder and the reading ladder alike.

The review door's `after` takes what only the workflow knows: the verdict
count `before` emitted, the fan-out ceiling it chose, which harness step ran
the session, and where Claude Code's transcript is. Each is a step output in
the YAML and a flag here, so the run log says where every number came from,
and the first three are refused absent rather than defaulted, an empty
string counting as absent: the pass is the branch that has to be reached, so
a number that did not arrive, from a renamed output or a skipped step, ends
the run red and not green.

History in on.history.md (solorepo's DR-171).
"""

